WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.770 --> 00:00:03.560
So Fastly's a CDN.

00:00:03.560 --> 00:00:05.435
Like I said, we're kind
of a next generation,

00:00:05.435 --> 00:00:07.690
although that's kind of
a cheesy marketing term.

00:00:07.690 --> 00:00:09.940
You probably actually use
us without knowing about us.

00:00:09.940 --> 00:00:12.210
This is some our customers.

00:00:12.210 --> 00:00:14.086
We've got Twitter on there, BBC.

00:00:14.086 --> 00:00:15.710
The British government
runs through us,

00:00:15.710 --> 00:00:20.020
which is kind of cool,
although a bit scary.

00:00:20.020 --> 00:00:22.350
This is also some
of our customers.

00:00:22.350 --> 00:00:24.440
I realize, actually, in
retrospect, some of those

00:00:24.440 --> 00:00:25.210
are duplicated.

00:00:25.210 --> 00:00:27.400
I promise you that's
me screwing up,

00:00:27.400 --> 00:00:30.751
not me trying to pad the stuff.

00:00:30.751 --> 00:00:32.820
This is going to be
less video oriented

00:00:32.820 --> 00:00:34.986
than some of the other
stuff, although we will shove

00:00:34.986 --> 00:00:36.379
some video stuff in at the end.

00:00:36.379 --> 00:00:38.420
This is more about just
like why we're different,

00:00:38.420 --> 00:00:42.410
why we did a CDN, and sort
of just go on from there.

00:00:42.410 --> 00:00:44.897
So yeah, if I rush through
stuff, I apologize.

00:00:44.897 --> 00:00:45.730
Grab me afterwards .

00:00:45.730 --> 00:00:47.313
I'm going to be at
the cocktail party,

00:00:47.313 --> 00:00:51.540
trying to surf my sort
of, whatever, sine waves--

00:00:51.540 --> 00:00:53.820
the things-- circadian
rhythms, that's the one.

00:00:53.820 --> 00:00:54.540
Sine waves?

00:00:57.700 --> 00:00:58.200
Yeah.

00:00:58.200 --> 00:01:01.230
So people, when we started
off, people asked us like, why?

00:01:01.230 --> 00:01:03.467
Why did you build a CDN?

00:01:03.467 --> 00:01:05.175
You've got Akamai--
they're the 800 pound

00:01:05.175 --> 00:01:07.907
gorilla in the room-- you've got
EdgeCast, you've got Limelight.

00:01:07.907 --> 00:01:08.990
Amazon is in the business.

00:01:08.990 --> 00:01:10.570
Like, why would you do this?

00:01:10.570 --> 00:01:11.986
And basically it's
because we were

00:01:11.986 --> 00:01:14.680
really unhappy with the way that
the CDN market was at the time.

00:01:14.680 --> 00:01:17.380
You didn't get a lot of
control, configuration

00:01:17.380 --> 00:01:18.140
was really crappy.

00:01:18.140 --> 00:01:19.640
When you made a
configuration change

00:01:19.640 --> 00:01:23.410
it could take, at best, minutes,
sometimes hours to do stuff.

00:01:23.410 --> 00:01:26.140
You don't have a lot of
control over your data.

00:01:26.140 --> 00:01:28.490
When you wanted to purge
stuff, again minutes or hours

00:01:28.490 --> 00:01:30.090
to change stuff
which, when you're

00:01:30.090 --> 00:01:32.380
dealing with a
modern content site,

00:01:32.380 --> 00:01:34.015
and you didn't get
a lot of visibility

00:01:34.015 --> 00:01:36.140
and you were locked into
these year-long contracts,

00:01:36.140 --> 00:01:37.110
it was crappy.

00:01:37.110 --> 00:01:39.590
It was just a really
crappy way of doing stuff

00:01:39.590 --> 00:01:42.800
and it didn't really fit in with
the way we do development now.

00:01:42.800 --> 00:01:45.300
Like yeah, sure, when we used
to have a release once a week,

00:01:45.300 --> 00:01:46.700
or once a month, that was fine.

00:01:46.700 --> 00:01:50.150
But now people do 10,
12, 20 releases a day.

00:01:50.150 --> 00:01:51.765
That didn't really
work that well.

00:01:51.765 --> 00:01:53.360
And also the support
was kind of crap.

00:01:53.360 --> 00:01:55.540
Like every time you phoned up
the support thing, you dreaded.

00:01:55.540 --> 00:01:57.350
You had to go through
your sales rep

00:01:57.350 --> 00:01:59.710
and then they would try
to upsell on something.

00:01:59.710 --> 00:02:01.140
And you'd be like, no, I
just want to figure out

00:02:01.140 --> 00:02:02.080
how to do this thing.

00:02:02.080 --> 00:02:03.220
I don't want to
have to pay for two

00:02:03.220 --> 00:02:04.750
hours of professional services.

00:02:04.750 --> 00:02:06.040
So we were like, enough.

00:02:06.040 --> 00:02:08.699
We will build our own.

00:02:08.699 --> 00:02:10.610
And it worked out.

00:02:10.610 --> 00:02:11.530
And this [INAUDIBLE].

00:02:11.530 --> 00:02:12.940
This is our pop map.

00:02:12.940 --> 00:02:15.500
It's actually
already out of date.

00:02:15.500 --> 00:02:18.500
Stockholm is already
up, Asaka is coming up,

00:02:18.500 --> 00:02:20.341
I think sometime soon.

00:02:20.341 --> 00:02:21.340
We're looking at Brazil.

00:02:21.340 --> 00:02:25.310
We're also looking at a bunch
of other places, Taipei, Seoul,

00:02:25.310 --> 00:02:26.210
South Africa.

00:02:26.210 --> 00:02:28.700
It's actually really easy
for us to bring up pops.

00:02:28.700 --> 00:02:30.640
So like, based on
market stuff, it

00:02:30.640 --> 00:02:32.752
could be ready you know--
if you're looking on

00:02:32.752 --> 00:02:34.210
here and going
well, you don't have

00:02:34.210 --> 00:02:36.709
a place where I need it, come
talk to us anyway because it's

00:02:36.709 --> 00:02:38.510
really easy for us
to bring up a pop,

00:02:38.510 --> 00:02:41.240
unless there's really odd,
local-- unless there's

00:02:41.240 --> 00:02:42.180
really odd local laws.

00:02:42.180 --> 00:02:44.930
Like Brazil is actually
a real pain in the arse,

00:02:44.930 --> 00:02:45.770
for various reasons.

00:02:45.770 --> 00:02:47.644
Come talk to me afterwards
when I'm drinking.

00:02:50.520 --> 00:02:53.430
Because it makes
me want to drink.

00:02:53.430 --> 00:02:56.560
So why would you use--
why would you use Fastly?

00:02:56.560 --> 00:02:58.910
This is the kind of sales event.

00:02:58.910 --> 00:03:01.680
When it started off like--
so what we started off doing,

00:03:01.680 --> 00:03:06.370
I suggested one of our slogans
should be "faster, cheaper,

00:03:06.370 --> 00:03:07.950
better, pick three."

00:03:07.950 --> 00:03:10.130
Turns out that's why I
should never be in marketing.

00:03:10.130 --> 00:03:12.020
But basically, we
can make your--

00:03:12.020 --> 00:03:14.279
we basically can make
your site faster,

00:03:14.279 --> 00:03:15.820
we can lower your
costs, and we could

00:03:15.820 --> 00:03:18.090
make your site more reliable.

00:03:18.090 --> 00:03:20.705
And we're basically
trying to lower

00:03:20.705 --> 00:03:23.570
your total cost of ownership.

00:03:23.570 --> 00:03:26.335
Total cost of ownership is
made up of multiple assets,

00:03:26.335 --> 00:03:27.850
and it's everything
from reliability

00:03:27.850 --> 00:03:30.145
to geographic
distribution, it's control,

00:03:30.145 --> 00:03:33.060
to visibility, to how fast
you can go and do stuff.

00:03:33.060 --> 00:03:37.620
And so really, we wanted
to be part of that view.

00:03:37.620 --> 00:03:39.470
We can reduce your
CAPEX and OPEX.

00:03:39.470 --> 00:03:41.330
By going through
us, you don't have

00:03:41.330 --> 00:03:43.450
to have as many origin servers.

00:03:43.450 --> 00:03:45.566
And so therefore,
your CAPEX is lower.

00:03:45.566 --> 00:03:46.940
And also, our
bandwidth costs are

00:03:46.940 --> 00:03:48.120
going to be lower than
yours because we've

00:03:48.120 --> 00:03:49.160
got economies and scale.

00:03:49.160 --> 00:03:53.090
Unless you're Google, in which
case-- but even Google use us,

00:03:53.090 --> 00:03:55.650
so that should
tell you something.

00:03:55.650 --> 00:03:58.340
And so, we can reduce
your OPEX as well.

00:03:58.340 --> 00:04:00.650
If you're using us, we've
got a really sweetheart deal

00:04:00.650 --> 00:04:03.690
with Google, when it comes
to egress from Google Cloud

00:04:03.690 --> 00:04:06.480
Storage, so that will
lower you Google costs.

00:04:06.480 --> 00:04:08.334
Thank you very much, Google.

00:04:08.334 --> 00:04:09.750
And go and talk
to our sales guys,

00:04:09.750 --> 00:04:12.814
they will tell you kind of
how much you'll be saving.

00:04:12.814 --> 00:04:14.230
You don't need as
much redundancy.

00:04:14.230 --> 00:04:16.700
You can have one data center
and we will take care of that.

00:04:16.700 --> 00:04:19.620
Or you don't need quite
as many data centers.

00:04:19.620 --> 00:04:21.750
We will reduce your bounce rate.

00:04:21.750 --> 00:04:24.290
The faster your site is and
the more reliable it is,

00:04:24.290 --> 00:04:26.280
the more likely people
are to come back.

00:04:26.280 --> 00:04:28.310
And because of that,
because of the faster rate

00:04:28.310 --> 00:04:30.360
we'll increase your page rank.

00:04:30.360 --> 00:04:32.470
And so that means more
people will come to you.

00:04:32.470 --> 00:04:34.602
We'll increase your
revenue because there

00:04:34.602 --> 00:04:36.560
is a really, really well
known link between how

00:04:36.560 --> 00:04:40.950
fast your site loads and how
much money people spend on it.

00:04:40.950 --> 00:04:42.700
You can intuitively
kind of grasp that.

00:04:42.700 --> 00:04:46.040
If you go to a site and you are
trying to buy something from it

00:04:46.040 --> 00:04:47.999
and it takes two seconds
for each page to load,

00:04:47.999 --> 00:04:49.581
you'll look at four
items and then go,

00:04:49.581 --> 00:04:50.900
you know what, I'm giving up.

00:04:50.900 --> 00:04:52.140
Whereas if it loads
really quickly,

00:04:52.140 --> 00:04:53.915
you're much more likely
to spend time around.

00:04:53.915 --> 00:04:55.336
And because of that,
people are going

00:04:55.336 --> 00:04:56.627
to spend more time on the site.

00:04:56.627 --> 00:04:58.500
So there's that neat symmetry.

00:04:58.500 --> 00:05:02.740
We'll reduce some stuff for you,
we'll increase other things.

00:05:02.740 --> 00:05:04.480
The reason why we
kind of can do this

00:05:04.480 --> 00:05:09.230
is that, back in the early
'90s-- sorry, early '90s.

00:05:09.230 --> 00:05:11.990
Back in the late
'90s early 2000s,

00:05:11.990 --> 00:05:14.660
really content was basically
into either static content--

00:05:14.660 --> 00:05:16.940
like images, CSS,
JavaScript, all that kind

00:05:16.940 --> 00:05:20.700
of stuff-- or it was fully
dynamic stuff, running

00:05:20.700 --> 00:05:22.840
[INAUDIBLE] scripts through
modperl, or like ASP,

00:05:22.840 --> 00:05:26.530
or PHP 3.0, for anybody
who's old school.

00:05:26.530 --> 00:05:29.177
And it was mostly-- those two
concepts, completely separate.

00:05:29.177 --> 00:05:31.510
It was either stuff was either
static or it was dynamic,

00:05:31.510 --> 00:05:34.650
and never the twain shall meet.

00:05:34.650 --> 00:05:37.185
Nowadays, we have this term--
we started using this term,

00:05:37.185 --> 00:05:39.560
"event-driven content" mostly
because we needed some sort

00:05:39.560 --> 00:05:41.290
of marketing term rather than
trying to explain it every

00:05:41.290 --> 00:05:41.940
time.

00:05:41.940 --> 00:05:44.550
But that's basically
stuff like content

00:05:44.550 --> 00:05:46.690
doesn't actually
change that often.

00:05:46.690 --> 00:05:49.300
So you're looking at stuff like
the front page of a newspaper

00:05:49.300 --> 00:05:50.369
or something like that.

00:05:50.369 --> 00:05:52.160
Everybody's looking at
the same front page,

00:05:52.160 --> 00:05:54.387
it's dynamically generated,
but realistically, it's

00:05:54.387 --> 00:05:56.720
sticking around for five
minutes or something like that.

00:05:56.720 --> 00:05:58.320
It doesn't actually
change that much.

00:05:58.320 --> 00:06:00.140
Something triggers it
that causes a change.

00:06:00.140 --> 00:06:03.320
Somebody publishes a new
story, stock market changes,

00:06:03.320 --> 00:06:05.990
a new event happens or
something like that.

00:06:05.990 --> 00:06:07.240
You get a new mail.

00:06:07.240 --> 00:06:10.530
All that stuff is event-driven,
it's not truly dynamic.

00:06:10.530 --> 00:06:11.961
So that's where we can come in.

00:06:11.961 --> 00:06:13.960
And that's actually a
huge amount of your stuff.

00:06:13.960 --> 00:06:16.000
And we can cache
that for you, then

00:06:16.000 --> 00:06:17.540
you can save yourself
a huge amount

00:06:17.540 --> 00:06:19.600
of-- you can make
your page a lot faster

00:06:19.600 --> 00:06:22.850
and you can save yourself
a huge amount of bandwidth.

00:06:22.850 --> 00:06:25.630
So statically kind of
stuff, we can drastically

00:06:25.630 --> 00:06:28.256
increase your cache hit ratio.

00:06:28.256 --> 00:06:30.130
Sorry, I just realized
the conversion of this

00:06:30.130 --> 00:06:32.254
from PowerPoint hasn't
actually gone as well as it,

00:06:32.254 --> 00:06:34.280
so stuff is going off.

00:06:34.280 --> 00:06:37.600
So with traditional
static stuff,

00:06:37.600 --> 00:06:40.490
we can cache it
really well for you.

00:06:40.490 --> 00:06:43.840
We use math-- we
call these commodity

00:06:43.840 --> 00:06:46.170
servers because they're
built of commodity parts,

00:06:46.170 --> 00:06:49.410
but they tend to
have 768 gigs of RAM,

00:06:49.410 --> 00:06:53.500
they tend to have terabytes
and terabytes of SSDs.

00:06:53.500 --> 00:06:57.300
We wrote our own file system.

00:06:57.300 --> 00:06:59.394
It seemed like a really
good idea at the time.

00:06:59.394 --> 00:07:02.060
This reason why we're doing that
is we don't need a regular file

00:07:02.060 --> 00:07:05.352
system, all we need to do is
put cached objects down on disk.

00:07:05.352 --> 00:07:06.810
And if we can look
after the file--

00:07:06.810 --> 00:07:08.518
we can look after the
SSD, we can get rid

00:07:08.518 --> 00:07:09.430
of all that overhead.

00:07:09.430 --> 00:07:12.430
The smaller the cached object
is on disk, the more stuff

00:07:12.430 --> 00:07:14.950
we can stick on the SSD.

00:07:14.950 --> 00:07:17.350
And if we look after the
SSD, instead of burning out

00:07:17.350 --> 00:07:19.320
after three months,
we've got SSDs

00:07:19.320 --> 00:07:21.580
which are now three years old.

00:07:21.580 --> 00:07:24.480
And the advantage of using SSDs
is that they go really, really

00:07:24.480 --> 00:07:25.010
fast.

00:07:25.010 --> 00:07:29.320
And I'm going to go into
that a little bit later on.

00:07:29.320 --> 00:07:31.100
We're really good for
this kind of stuff.

00:07:31.100 --> 00:07:34.100
You may notice at the bottom,
that's us, we're really fast.

00:07:34.100 --> 00:07:37.660
Catchpoint is like a
monitoring and testing service.

00:07:37.660 --> 00:07:39.200
Also, look how smooth we are.

00:07:39.200 --> 00:07:42.497
So we're calm, ordered people.

00:07:42.497 --> 00:07:43.830
So then there's dynamic content.

00:07:43.830 --> 00:07:46.610
This is purely dynamic content.

00:07:46.610 --> 00:07:49.470
I think Akamai calls this DSA,
dynamic site acceleration.

00:07:49.470 --> 00:07:52.330
They sell it as an add on.

00:07:52.330 --> 00:07:55.232
I'm not trying to bag on
Akamai, they're big for a reason

00:07:55.232 --> 00:07:57.190
and they do some really,
really important work,

00:07:57.190 --> 00:08:00.590
just using the kind of
terms that they use.

00:08:00.590 --> 00:08:03.370
Dynamic content,
you can't cache it.

00:08:03.370 --> 00:08:06.020
Let's say it's like the current
time or something like that,

00:08:06.020 --> 00:08:06.811
you can't cache it.

00:08:06.811 --> 00:08:09.870
Or somebody's very,
very personal data,

00:08:09.870 --> 00:08:10.950
you can't cache it.

00:08:10.950 --> 00:08:12.940
That's fine, we
will accelerate it.

00:08:12.940 --> 00:08:15.130
And the reason why
we can do that is you

00:08:15.130 --> 00:08:18.260
can have an initial connection
to one of pop servers which

00:08:18.260 --> 00:08:20.320
is closer to your users.

00:08:20.320 --> 00:08:23.910
The TCP, the three way handshake
that TCP does at the start

00:08:23.910 --> 00:08:25.864
will be a lot quicker
because the latency

00:08:25.864 --> 00:08:28.280
between your user and our pop
is going to be a lot better.

00:08:28.280 --> 00:08:30.960
And then, between
our pops, we keep

00:08:30.960 --> 00:08:32.590
keepalives between our pops.

00:08:32.590 --> 00:08:35.210
And we know the best
route between them,

00:08:35.210 --> 00:08:37.018
and at the other
end, we will then

00:08:37.018 --> 00:08:38.309
go back to your origin servers.

00:08:38.309 --> 00:08:39.900
And again, the
three way handshake

00:08:39.900 --> 00:08:44.169
will be a lot faster between
what we call our shield node

00:08:44.169 --> 00:08:47.150
and between your origin,
that will be a lot faster.

00:08:47.150 --> 00:08:51.060
And also we can try and keep
a keepalive back to you.

00:08:51.060 --> 00:08:53.240
So even when we can't
cache something,

00:08:53.240 --> 00:08:54.760
we will make it faster.

00:08:54.760 --> 00:08:57.330
And for us though, instead of
being an additional product

00:08:57.330 --> 00:09:00.582
that you buy and it's like an
extra line item on your bill,

00:09:00.582 --> 00:09:02.040
for us it's the
worst case scenario

00:09:02.040 --> 00:09:04.620
when we can't do anything else.

00:09:04.620 --> 00:09:06.630
We will try as hard as
possible to cache stuff,

00:09:06.630 --> 00:09:09.030
but if we can't cache it, we
will still make it faster.

00:09:09.030 --> 00:09:11.190
And that's just considered,
well, in the worst case

00:09:11.190 --> 00:09:13.030
scenario, we'll do that.

00:09:13.030 --> 00:09:17.660
So I won't read the
quotes from the side.

00:09:17.660 --> 00:09:19.590
So once again, we rock.

00:09:19.590 --> 00:09:22.120
I'm not entirely sure what
happened on February the 21st,

00:09:22.120 --> 00:09:24.851
apparently everybody
had a bad day.

00:09:24.851 --> 00:09:25.350
Yeah.

00:09:25.350 --> 00:09:29.650
So we just like showing
these graphs, they're nice.

00:09:29.650 --> 00:09:31.920
So then we go on to this
third type of caching.

00:09:31.920 --> 00:09:35.090
And this is what we call
event-driven content.

00:09:35.090 --> 00:09:39.160
And that's stuff like API,
mobile stuff, news articles,

00:09:39.160 --> 00:09:40.910
user generated
content like wikis.

00:09:40.910 --> 00:09:43.980
We actually kind of started
off as part of Wikia.

00:09:43.980 --> 00:09:46.917
So if you've ever
used like-- you

00:09:46.917 --> 00:09:48.250
may or may not know about Wikia.

00:09:48.250 --> 00:09:49.916
But basically, if you
go to a game site,

00:09:49.916 --> 00:09:52.350
or if you go to Wikipedia,
if you're into "Star Wars,"

00:09:52.350 --> 00:09:54.683
or if you go to the Muppet
Wiki if you're into Muppets--

00:09:54.683 --> 00:09:58.140
and if you're not, I don't
really want to know you--

00:09:58.140 --> 00:10:00.260
then you use Wikia.

00:10:00.260 --> 00:10:02.730
And in turn, you're
actually using us.

00:10:02.730 --> 00:10:04.180
And you think about a wiki.

00:10:04.180 --> 00:10:05.900
Like media Wikia is
not the best piece

00:10:05.900 --> 00:10:06.840
of written piece of software.

00:10:06.840 --> 00:10:09.340
It's great, but it's not the
best written piece of software.

00:10:09.340 --> 00:10:11.040
It's really slow.

00:10:11.040 --> 00:10:13.642
And you think about it,
it's a very, very long tail.

00:10:13.642 --> 00:10:15.600
And if you had to do kind
of traditional stuff,

00:10:15.600 --> 00:10:17.936
baking all the pages and
loading them into a CVN,

00:10:17.936 --> 00:10:19.560
then that would be
terrible because you

00:10:19.560 --> 00:10:20.510
would be uploading
a lot of stuff

00:10:20.510 --> 00:10:22.384
and you don't know if
it's going to get used.

00:10:22.384 --> 00:10:23.820
Nobody's really
necessarily going

00:10:23.820 --> 00:10:26.700
to look at the list of the
names of every single Luke

00:10:26.700 --> 00:10:29.810
Skywalker's lightsabers,
if you gave them names.

00:10:29.810 --> 00:10:33.930
I'm picking an example
completely at random here.

00:10:33.930 --> 00:10:39.530
But then, when a wiki
changes-- WoW wiki gets

00:10:39.530 --> 00:10:42.764
updated a lot when a new
WoW expansion comes out--

00:10:42.764 --> 00:10:44.180
then stuff changes
really quickly.

00:10:44.180 --> 00:10:46.080
You can't be waiting
eight minutes,

00:10:46.080 --> 00:10:48.649
let alone eight hours
for a page you updated.

00:10:48.649 --> 00:10:50.690
eCommerce sites are
obviously a really big thing.

00:10:50.690 --> 00:10:52.814
They're kind of just like
content publishing sites,

00:10:52.814 --> 00:10:54.810
but when prices change
and inventories change,

00:10:54.810 --> 00:10:56.790
you really need to
update the page quickly.

00:10:56.790 --> 00:10:58.330
Sports scores are another thing.

00:10:58.330 --> 00:11:03.560
They don't change for ages, like
the Dodgers game last night,

00:11:03.560 --> 00:11:05.970
it doesn't change for ages,
but then when it does change,

00:11:05.970 --> 00:11:07.750
you really need
to update quickly.

00:11:07.750 --> 00:11:09.410
So yeah, people like that.

00:11:09.410 --> 00:11:10.680
We've got "The Guardian."

00:11:10.680 --> 00:11:12.620
I always bring up "The Guardian"
because it my first sales,

00:11:12.620 --> 00:11:13.670
even though I'm an engineer.

00:11:13.670 --> 00:11:14.544
It was kind of weird.

00:11:14.544 --> 00:11:16.090
It was a bittersweet
moment for me.

00:11:16.090 --> 00:11:17.590
I kind of felt a
bit of my soul die.

00:11:20.240 --> 00:11:23.850
And Shazam and all that kind
of content, stuff like that.

00:11:23.850 --> 00:11:24.584
And Conde Nast.

00:11:24.584 --> 00:11:26.000
And so Wired run
through us, which

00:11:26.000 --> 00:11:28.847
gave me a massive geek thrill.

00:11:28.847 --> 00:11:31.430
So the features that really kind
of do that, we have real-time

00:11:31.430 --> 00:11:31.929
purging.

00:11:31.929 --> 00:11:35.140
When you purge content from
us, you can say, be gone.

00:11:35.140 --> 00:11:36.320
And we will be like, OK.

00:11:36.320 --> 00:11:38.930
And in 150 milliseconds,
worldwide, it's gone.

00:11:38.930 --> 00:11:40.130
And that's huge.

00:11:40.130 --> 00:11:42.930
We actually have three
different ways of purging stuff.

00:11:42.930 --> 00:11:45.039
You can either purge
by URL, you can

00:11:45.039 --> 00:11:47.330
purge by something we call
surrogate key purging, which

00:11:47.330 --> 00:11:49.560
is when you can tag
content with multiple keys.

00:11:49.560 --> 00:11:52.170
So each bit of content can have
multiple keys attached to it.

00:11:52.170 --> 00:11:55.260
And each key can have multiple
objects attached to it.

00:11:55.260 --> 00:11:57.682
So it's a really handy way
of grouping stuff together.

00:11:57.682 --> 00:11:59.765
So you know, if you've got
up at the obvious thing

00:11:59.765 --> 00:12:03.930
is like if you've got an article
on site, you've got the page.

00:12:03.930 --> 00:12:06.560
You've got one or more pages
with one more images or assets

00:12:06.560 --> 00:12:08.750
in it, you tag them all
with the same article ID.

00:12:08.750 --> 00:12:11.170
If that article needs to
get updated, you purge it,

00:12:11.170 --> 00:12:12.790
all those things are gone.

00:12:12.790 --> 00:12:14.360
You can also tag
with the author.

00:12:14.360 --> 00:12:15.860
So if you're "The New York
Time" and you find out

00:12:15.860 --> 00:12:17.780
one of your authors has
being plagiarizing everything

00:12:17.780 --> 00:12:19.780
and you need to purge
everything by that author,

00:12:19.780 --> 00:12:22.340
do one call and it's all gone.

00:12:22.340 --> 00:12:23.980
We do real-time logs.

00:12:23.980 --> 00:12:25.562
This would be
useless-- you caching

00:12:25.562 --> 00:12:27.520
stuff would be absolutely
useless if you didn't

00:12:27.520 --> 00:12:28.846
know what was going on with it.

00:12:28.846 --> 00:12:30.970
And so we can stream logs
back to you in real-time.

00:12:30.970 --> 00:12:33.700
And again, we'll go in
to a bit of that later.

00:12:33.700 --> 00:12:37.870
Kind of skipping over stuff
just in case I go over time.

00:12:37.870 --> 00:12:39.910
We have this origin
shield, which is this idea

00:12:39.910 --> 00:12:44.030
that you can do
multi-tiered caching.

00:12:44.030 --> 00:12:47.580
So basically, stuff comes in
to one of our edge servers,

00:12:47.580 --> 00:12:50.310
and then we will reroute
any cache misses back

00:12:50.310 --> 00:12:53.830
to an origin pop,
which is as close as

00:12:53.830 --> 00:12:55.617
possible to your origin.

00:12:55.617 --> 00:12:57.450
And then that will
shield all your requests.

00:12:57.450 --> 00:12:59.700
It will make sure that you
only have one request of it

00:12:59.700 --> 00:13:01.260
going back to your origin.

00:13:01.260 --> 00:13:03.500
It protects you from
stampeding herds.

00:13:03.500 --> 00:13:06.240
And also, like I said, it gives
this much, much faster kind

00:13:06.240 --> 00:13:08.160
of response, because
we know the best

00:13:08.160 --> 00:13:10.110
route between all our pops.

00:13:10.110 --> 00:13:12.570
And we can keep keepalives
between our shield pops

00:13:12.570 --> 00:13:14.390
and with the origin.

00:13:14.390 --> 00:13:16.170
And then we also do
request collapsing.

00:13:16.170 --> 00:13:17.640
If two people are
coming and asking

00:13:17.640 --> 00:13:19.410
for the same bit of
content at the same time,

00:13:19.410 --> 00:13:21.410
we will recognize that
and then collapse it down

00:13:21.410 --> 00:13:22.210
into one request.

00:13:22.210 --> 00:13:24.000
So again, just when
you purge something,

00:13:24.000 --> 00:13:26.955
you don't see this
massive spike in traffic,

00:13:26.955 --> 00:13:28.580
which would mean you
still have to keep

00:13:28.580 --> 00:13:31.150
this kind of redundant
hardware around.

00:13:31.150 --> 00:13:34.790
So the benefits of that
should be pretty obvious.

00:13:34.790 --> 00:13:38.500
Your site is a lot
faster, you can handle

00:13:38.500 --> 00:13:39.840
unpredictable traffic spikes.

00:13:39.840 --> 00:13:45.849
If you're the "Boston
Globe" and you finally

00:13:45.849 --> 00:13:47.890
get into the World Series
or something like that,

00:13:47.890 --> 00:13:51.850
then you're going to see a
massive spike in traffic.

00:13:51.850 --> 00:13:54.160
If you're the "Guardian"
newspaper and a royal baby

00:13:54.160 --> 00:13:57.220
is born, then that's
going to happen as well.

00:13:57.220 --> 00:13:59.754
We also give you this
real-time configurability.

00:13:59.754 --> 00:14:02.170
You can change your config and
have it deployed everywhere

00:14:02.170 --> 00:14:04.980
in like, two seconds, worldwide.

00:14:04.980 --> 00:14:06.260
Everything is API driven.

00:14:06.260 --> 00:14:07.990
We do have the UI
on top of that,

00:14:07.990 --> 00:14:11.880
but everything is API
driven because we don't know

00:14:11.880 --> 00:14:13.220
how you want to configure us.

00:14:13.220 --> 00:14:16.100
If you want to build this into
your puppet chain or your Chef

00:14:16.100 --> 00:14:17.560
or whatever, then that's cool.

00:14:17.560 --> 00:14:19.820
If you want to rig
us into something,

00:14:19.820 --> 00:14:21.370
that's absolutely fine.

00:14:21.370 --> 00:14:23.950
We want to work with
you how you work,

00:14:23.950 --> 00:14:26.822
no force you to work how we do.

00:14:26.822 --> 00:14:29.280
And we're trying to give you
total control of your content.

00:14:29.280 --> 00:14:30.960
You can send us
back cache edges.

00:14:30.960 --> 00:14:33.380
You can override
everything in our UI.

00:14:33.380 --> 00:14:35.532
You can actually
upload edge scripting

00:14:35.532 --> 00:14:36.490
to override everything.

00:14:36.490 --> 00:14:39.300
We don't know how
you use your content,

00:14:39.300 --> 00:14:41.470
so we want to give you
the control to do stuff.

00:14:41.470 --> 00:14:43.919
It's also kind of proactive
laziness on our part.

00:14:43.919 --> 00:14:45.960
The more you can do stuff,
the less we have to do

00:14:45.960 --> 00:14:47.370
and that's really great.

00:14:47.370 --> 00:14:48.680
Especially back when
we were starting off,

00:14:48.680 --> 00:14:51.140
it meant I could actually sleep
and spend more time welding

00:14:51.140 --> 00:14:52.880
giant metal ears.

00:14:52.880 --> 00:14:56.510
And so we're trying
to lower your CAPEX.

00:14:56.510 --> 00:14:59.010
And basically, the
way we were able to do

00:14:59.010 --> 00:15:01.390
this, when we started
off, people were kind of,

00:15:01.390 --> 00:15:03.240
how are you going to
compete against Akamai?

00:15:03.240 --> 00:15:04.780
How are you're going to
compete against EdgeCast?

00:15:04.780 --> 00:15:06.730
How are you going to be
compete against Limelight?

00:15:06.730 --> 00:15:08.700
They're so much bigger, they
have economies of scale,

00:15:08.700 --> 00:15:10.310
you're going to
be too expensive.

00:15:10.310 --> 00:15:12.170
And basically,
they were all built

00:15:12.170 --> 00:15:14.840
on-- you may notice the
back of my thing says,

00:15:14.840 --> 00:15:19.450
life is too short
to use and 90 CVN.

00:15:19.450 --> 00:15:22.219
Rather than build a network
back in the early 2000s

00:15:22.219 --> 00:15:23.760
when the internet
was very different.

00:15:23.760 --> 00:15:25.110
It was crappy.

00:15:25.110 --> 00:15:27.810
Late '90s, early 2000s,
things were really crappy.

00:15:27.810 --> 00:15:29.170
Now, things are a lot better.

00:15:29.170 --> 00:15:32.370
And we can do stuff by
building much fewer pops

00:15:32.370 --> 00:15:35.790
at internet backbones,
rather than at local ISPs.

00:15:35.790 --> 00:15:38.130
We can build much larger pops.

00:15:38.130 --> 00:15:39.454
We can use SSDs.

00:15:39.454 --> 00:15:41.370
The big problem with
using regular hard drives

00:15:41.370 --> 00:15:45.650
is the more space you've got,
the slower the averaging sheet

00:15:45.650 --> 00:15:49.180
time is going to be because
the sheet time is linear.

00:15:49.180 --> 00:15:52.700
Sorry, is linear
compared to size.

00:15:52.700 --> 00:15:54.990
Because it just takes
wider-- you know,

00:15:54.990 --> 00:15:57.692
it's just a spinning bit of
rust with a head moving over it.

00:15:57.692 --> 00:15:59.150
So it's going to
take longer to do.

00:15:59.150 --> 00:16:01.690
With an SSD, lookup times
are completely constant.

00:16:01.690 --> 00:16:06.232
So we can have much, much larger
pops, much, much larger drives,

00:16:06.232 --> 00:16:08.190
which means it's much
more likely your stuff is

00:16:08.190 --> 00:16:10.350
going to be in cache.

00:16:10.350 --> 00:16:12.350
SSDs is a really high
cost per gigabyte,

00:16:12.350 --> 00:16:14.650
but they're really
low cost per IOP.

00:16:14.650 --> 00:16:18.190
I mean, you were talking
about 70,000 IOPs

00:16:18.190 --> 00:16:20.290
per second compared
to like seven

00:16:20.290 --> 00:16:23.670
for a really high end hard
drive or something like that.

00:16:23.670 --> 00:16:25.999
So we can get a lot more
performance out of our pops.

00:16:25.999 --> 00:16:27.540
We get a lot more
efficiency and then

00:16:27.540 --> 00:16:30.390
we can pass those costs over.

00:16:30.390 --> 00:16:32.290
You increased
cache-hit ratio just

00:16:32.290 --> 00:16:35.430
means you get less traffic back
to you, which is really good.

00:16:35.430 --> 00:16:37.790
And if you think
about it, we can

00:16:37.790 --> 00:16:40.560
increase your cache-hit
ratio from 90% to say 95%,

00:16:40.560 --> 00:16:41.946
it doesn't sound
that impressive.

00:16:41.946 --> 00:16:43.320
But actually what
we've just done

00:16:43.320 --> 00:16:45.060
is halved your
origin traffic back.

00:16:45.060 --> 00:16:47.610
So that means you can get
rid of half your servers.

00:16:47.610 --> 00:16:50.079
And not only that but then
all that content-- like,

00:16:50.079 --> 00:16:51.620
serving stuff from
the cache is going

00:16:51.620 --> 00:16:54.160
to be so much faster than having
to go back to your origin,

00:16:54.160 --> 00:16:56.280
you running whatever
app, rails app,

00:16:56.280 --> 00:16:59.670
whatever PHP in the background,
and then go back again.

00:16:59.670 --> 00:17:02.686
That request has gone from
being 500 milliseconds a second

00:17:02.686 --> 00:17:04.310
or something like
that, it's come back,

00:17:04.310 --> 00:17:06.359
we're responding to it
within 20 milliseconds.

00:17:06.359 --> 00:17:08.756
So your page is going
to be a lot, lot faster.

00:17:08.756 --> 00:17:11.089
And then I've already kind
of talked about origin shield

00:17:11.089 --> 00:17:12.410
quite a lot, so I won't.

00:17:15.130 --> 00:17:16.680
And we try to give
you more control.

00:17:16.680 --> 00:17:18.890
We should be integrated
into your development cycle.

00:17:18.890 --> 00:17:20.490
When people think
about Cloud, when

00:17:20.490 --> 00:17:22.115
they think about
Google Cloud platform,

00:17:22.115 --> 00:17:25.410
when they think about AWS and
stuff like that, the reason why

00:17:25.410 --> 00:17:27.119
it took off is
because people can--

00:17:27.119 --> 00:17:28.842
it fits in with the
way you do stuff.

00:17:28.842 --> 00:17:31.300
It doesn't feel like there's
a vendor you're throwing stuff

00:17:31.300 --> 00:17:32.000
into.

00:17:32.000 --> 00:17:36.135
It feels like it's much more
part of your development stack.

00:17:36.135 --> 00:17:38.130
OK, that worked.

00:17:38.130 --> 00:17:40.867
We wanted to be
complete self-service.

00:17:40.867 --> 00:17:42.450
You shouldn't have
to phone up and get

00:17:42.450 --> 00:17:44.750
hold of professional services
just to change something.

00:17:44.750 --> 00:17:46.291
When you push a
configuration change,

00:17:46.291 --> 00:17:47.530
it should happen in seconds.

00:17:47.530 --> 00:17:49.230
All configurations
should be versioned,

00:17:49.230 --> 00:17:50.950
so you should be
able to roll back.

00:17:50.950 --> 00:17:52.700
And between those two
things, it gives you

00:17:52.700 --> 00:17:55.320
a lot more power to do
stuff and a lot more freedom

00:17:55.320 --> 00:17:56.540
to experiment.

00:17:56.540 --> 00:18:00.140
If you made a change
and it took five hours

00:18:00.140 --> 00:18:02.400
to sort of propagate, and
then when you finished,

00:18:02.400 --> 00:18:05.390
it was screwed up,
you'd be like, oh god,

00:18:05.390 --> 00:18:06.140
that's really bad.

00:18:06.140 --> 00:18:08.265
Now I've got to make a
change and wait another five

00:18:08.265 --> 00:18:09.405
hours for it to fix itself.

00:18:09.405 --> 00:18:11.780
And you're going to be much
less likely to change things.

00:18:11.780 --> 00:18:13.010
You're going to be
much more worried.

00:18:13.010 --> 00:18:14.510
It's going to be a
lot more fragile.

00:18:14.510 --> 00:18:16.362
You can make a change,
notice immediately

00:18:16.362 --> 00:18:18.820
from the real-time feedback
from the analytics and the logs

00:18:18.820 --> 00:18:21.040
that something's gone wrong
and immediately either rollback

00:18:21.040 --> 00:18:23.360
or make push the fix, you're
much more likely to go,

00:18:23.360 --> 00:18:25.154
OK, I'm going to
try other stuff.

00:18:25.154 --> 00:18:26.820
I'm going to try and
play around and see

00:18:26.820 --> 00:18:29.896
what more stuff I can
move t the edge servers.

00:18:29.896 --> 00:18:32.020
And then that's going to
be more beneficial to you.

00:18:32.020 --> 00:18:34.510
So we were trying--
we really wanted

00:18:34.510 --> 00:18:37.280
to create a system in
which you could do that.

00:18:37.280 --> 00:18:40.409
As I said, all functionality is
available via the API or a UI.

00:18:40.409 --> 00:18:42.450
And we've got this really
powerful edge scripting

00:18:42.450 --> 00:18:44.408
based on something called
varnish configuration

00:18:44.408 --> 00:18:45.450
language or VCL.

00:18:45.450 --> 00:18:47.765
VCL is basically a domain
specific language that

00:18:47.765 --> 00:18:50.220
lets you do anything with HTP.

00:18:50.220 --> 00:18:51.570
It's really, really powerful.

00:18:54.520 --> 00:18:55.920
So you can do a lot more stuff.

00:18:55.920 --> 00:18:57.970
You can move a lot more
content to the edge.

00:18:57.970 --> 00:18:59.178
And we've got stuff on there.

00:18:59.178 --> 00:19:01.200
We've got edge
authorization and Paywall

00:19:01.200 --> 00:19:03.650
We can do stuff based on
Geo IP and Geo restrictions.

00:19:03.650 --> 00:19:05.275
So if you've got
content that shouldn't

00:19:05.275 --> 00:19:08.877
be shown to some people but
should be shown to other,

00:19:08.877 --> 00:19:10.460
you can do that
through authorization.

00:19:10.460 --> 00:19:12.580
You can do mobile
device detection.

00:19:12.580 --> 00:19:14.330
You can do shopper
prioritization.

00:19:14.330 --> 00:19:16.370
It's when, you know,
if your origin server,

00:19:16.370 --> 00:19:19.370
your credit card payment
server is pretty loaded

00:19:19.370 --> 00:19:22.450
during the holiday period,
you can sit there and go,

00:19:22.450 --> 00:19:24.709
OK, well we'll just show up
a warning, a page saying,

00:19:24.709 --> 00:19:27.000
we're really sorry, your
order is very important to us,

00:19:27.000 --> 00:19:28.730
you'll be able to pay soon.

00:19:28.730 --> 00:19:30.854
But then you can also look
and ago, oh, this person

00:19:30.854 --> 00:19:31.563
has the VIP flag.

00:19:31.563 --> 00:19:33.562
Or this person's got like
$10,000 worth of stuff

00:19:33.562 --> 00:19:34.610
in their shopping basket.

00:19:34.610 --> 00:19:37.220
We're going to silently move
them off to the VIP server,

00:19:37.220 --> 00:19:39.760
and just allowance them
to go through as well.

00:19:39.760 --> 00:19:41.910
We can do all your
load balancing for you.

00:19:41.910 --> 00:19:44.580
Our CO is very, very
much against F5s.

00:19:44.580 --> 00:19:47.119
He really hates them, so he
basically wanted that built in.

00:19:47.119 --> 00:19:49.452
And we can do stuff like data
collection stuff, Internet

00:19:49.452 --> 00:19:50.230
of Things stuff.

00:19:50.230 --> 00:19:52.550
That's less relevant to
the rendering people here,

00:19:52.550 --> 00:19:55.890
but I thought I'd
mention it anyway.

00:19:55.890 --> 00:19:57.970
And this is kind of
an example of VCL.

00:19:57.970 --> 00:20:01.500
This is a way of having
a different edition

00:20:01.500 --> 00:20:03.949
for your content.

00:20:03.949 --> 00:20:05.990
So like, if somebody's
coming from international,

00:20:05.990 --> 00:20:07.140
somebody's coming
from mobile and stuff,

00:20:07.140 --> 00:20:08.010
you could redirect around.

00:20:08.010 --> 00:20:09.384
It's kind of a
contrived example,

00:20:09.384 --> 00:20:13.636
but it's in here for
illustrative purposes.

00:20:13.636 --> 00:20:17.710
Like I said, content purging
happens in 150 milliseconds.

00:20:17.710 --> 00:20:19.250
You can purge by URL.

00:20:19.250 --> 00:20:22.327
You have complete control over
what is defined as a cache key,

00:20:22.327 --> 00:20:24.410
so you can make some stuff
unique, some stuff not.

00:20:24.410 --> 00:20:26.284
So that can include,
say, somebody's language

00:20:26.284 --> 00:20:29.030
which they prefer to speak, or
whether or not the URL has URL

00:20:29.030 --> 00:20:30.430
parameters or something in it.

00:20:30.430 --> 00:20:32.360
And you can purge by
this surrogate key stuff

00:20:32.360 --> 00:20:33.160
that I was talking about.

00:20:33.160 --> 00:20:34.784
Sorry, I'm rushing
because I'm noticing

00:20:34.784 --> 00:20:36.820
the clock's ticking down.

00:20:36.820 --> 00:20:40.162
And like I said, we want to
give you more visibility.

00:20:40.162 --> 00:20:42.620
So we've got real-time logging
with completely customizable

00:20:42.620 --> 00:20:43.140
logs.

00:20:43.140 --> 00:20:45.780
We give you real-time stats,
we give you historical stats.

00:20:45.780 --> 00:20:47.530
And this gives you
this greater confidence

00:20:47.530 --> 00:20:48.405
being able to change.

00:20:48.405 --> 00:20:50.070
And the more
confidence you've got,

00:20:50.070 --> 00:20:51.300
the more stuff you're
going to cache.

00:20:51.300 --> 00:20:53.300
The more cache and stuff
you're caching with us,

00:20:53.300 --> 00:20:55.580
the more you owe us money,
and that makes us happy.

00:20:55.580 --> 00:20:56.660
That was a joke.

00:20:56.660 --> 00:20:57.436
But it's true.

00:20:57.436 --> 00:20:58.310
But it's also a joke.

00:21:01.325 --> 00:21:02.450
I probably scared you away.

00:21:02.450 --> 00:21:04.783
This is like an example, and
I don't think I've probably

00:21:04.783 --> 00:21:06.600
got time to go through it.

00:21:06.600 --> 00:21:07.490
We can cache content.

00:21:07.490 --> 00:21:10.030
Even if you've got tracking
cookies inside your content,

00:21:10.030 --> 00:21:13.005
we can cache that.

00:21:13.005 --> 00:21:14.706
You can track people
through the site.

00:21:14.706 --> 00:21:16.080
Normally, a tracking
cookie would

00:21:16.080 --> 00:21:22.440
be unique to a user, that
means you can't really--

00:21:22.440 --> 00:21:25.079
that means you can't
really cache that at all.

00:21:25.079 --> 00:21:27.120
What we can do is generate
the cookie at the edge

00:21:27.120 --> 00:21:29.370
and then track it back, and
then send it back via log.

00:21:29.370 --> 00:21:31.680
So even if you've got a
site with a tracking cookie,

00:21:31.680 --> 00:21:33.770
you can still cache it.

00:21:33.770 --> 00:21:36.490
You can precisely
follow video viewing,

00:21:36.490 --> 00:21:40.100
if you're using HLS, or HDS, or
dash, or something like that,

00:21:40.100 --> 00:21:42.360
each of those is video
separated into chunks,

00:21:42.360 --> 00:21:44.700
so you can fire a log
event for each chunk.

00:21:44.700 --> 00:21:47.650
You can also see how many bytes
we sent back versus the content

00:21:47.650 --> 00:21:50.500
length of the chunk, and say
the session ID or the user

00:21:50.500 --> 00:21:52.140
ID if the user is logged in.

00:21:52.140 --> 00:21:55.787
So then, if you're a large
specialist video site--

00:21:55.787 --> 00:21:58.120
so for example, they're not
actually a customer of ours,

00:21:58.120 --> 00:21:59.495
but they did a
thing which showed

00:21:59.495 --> 00:22:02.040
that people tend to watch the
first 30 seconds of videos,

00:22:02.040 --> 00:22:03.540
skip about three
minutes in and then

00:22:03.540 --> 00:22:06.044
skip to the end-- you could
see that through the logging.

00:22:06.044 --> 00:22:07.710
They're not actually
a customer of ours,

00:22:07.710 --> 00:22:11.210
but they wrote a really
interesting essay on it.

00:22:11.210 --> 00:22:13.900
And this was kind of,
this is other stuff

00:22:13.900 --> 00:22:15.790
